This%20paper%20presents%20tube-based%20Model%20Predictive%20Control%20%28MPC%29%20for%20the%20path%20and%20velocity%20tracking%20of%20an%20autonomous%20articulated%20vehicle.%20The%20target%20platform%20of%20this%20study%20is%20an%20autonomous%20articulated%20vehicle%20with%20a%20non-steerable%20axle.%20Consequently%2C%20the%20articulation%20angle%20and%20wheel%20torque%20input%20are%20determined%20by%20the%20tube-based%20MPC.%20The%20proposed%20MPC%20aims%20to%20achieve%20two%20objectives%3A%20minimizing%20path%20tracking%20error%20and%20enhancing%20robustness%20to%20disturbances.%20Furthermore%2C%20the%20lateral%20stability%20of%20the%20autonomous%20articulated%20vehicle%20is%20considered%20to%20reflect%20its%20dynamic%20characteristics.%20The%20vehicle%20model%20for%20the%20MPC%20is%20formulated%20using%20local%20linearization%20to%20minimize%20modeling%20errors.%20The%20reference%20state%20is%20determined%20using%20a%20virtual%20controller%20based%20on%20the%20linear%20quadratic%20regulator%20to%20provide%20the%20optimal%20reference%20for%20the%20MPC%20solver.%20The%20proposed%20algorithm%20was%20evaluated%20through%20a%20simulation%20study%20with%20base%20algorithms%20under%20noise%20injection%20into%20the%20sensor%20signal.%20Simulation%20results%20demonstrate%20that%20the%20proposed%20algorithm%20achieved%20the%20smallest%20path%20tracking%20error%2C%20compared%20to%20the%20base%20algorithms.%20Additionally%2C%20the%20proposed%20algorithm%20demonstrated%20robustness%20to%20external%20noise%20for%20multiple%20signals." title="Email">
